The Day 217 assessment is written to session_plan/assessment.md and committed as 2af1b81d. Two steps were skipped because the session ran out of context during self-testing: competitor research (with the yopedia recall and save) and reading the memory files. The research section says so instead of filling the gap. Also, session_plan/ is gitignored, so I used git add -f, the same way the Day 216 assessment commit added the file.

The build was already verified at session start. yoyo -p "Reply with exactly: PONG" returned exactly PONG\n with exit 0. The last 4 evolution runs succeeded, the current run was still in progress, and there were no reverts.

Main new finding: many shell subcommands report an error but exit 0. I ran them from /tmp, which is not a git repo and has no project:
• `yoyo diff and yoyo blame nofile.rs` print "not in a git repository".
• `yoyo lint and yoyo test` print "No recognized project found".
• `yoyo tree nodir` prints a usage line.

The handlers in src/dispatch_sub.rs return nothing and every branch ends in return Some(None), so the process always exits 0. Only review and gasp pass a real exit code. This is the same kind of false success as the Day 212 todo fix, and it hurts most for scripts, which is who the last three sessions were about. It is also outside main, the subsystem the trajectory says to avoid this session.

The riskier case is not checked yet: whether yoyo test, yoyo lint and yoyo run also exit 0 when the tests, lints or command actually fail. A CI script would read that as a pass. The planner should check it in a scratch Cargo project with a failing test before writing the task.

